 Commands that differ from vi

  Ctrl-F/Ctrl-B over long lines - Keep two lines of the page on screen (one on a screen of 4 text rows, none on
  fewer) however many rows they take. Vim keeps fewer when the lines kept and the longer of the lines next to them
  take more than TEXT_ROWS - 2 rows. Lines that fit a row page as in vim. See vim-gaps.md.

  j and k over Tabs - The column j and k keep is a byte column, as the editor shows each byte in one cell (a Tab
  as '>'). Vim's is a screen column: a Tab reaches the next multiple of 8 (and the cursor on one counts its last cell
  in normal mode), a control character takes 2 cells (^X) and a byte that is not UTF-8 4 (<80>): ' de' and ' abc'
  with 0r<Tab>j end on column 3 of ' abc' in vim, on column 0 here. See vim-gaps.md.

  Counts on i, a, A, o and O - A Backspace over text from before the typing, or a Delete, drops the count: the text
  goes in once. Vim types those keys again with the rest, each deleting as far as it can: 3A<BS>xy<Esc> on 'abc'
  gives 'abxxxy' in vim, 'abxy' here, and 3ix<Del><Esc> 'xxx' in vim, 'xbc' here. Copies that do not fit the text
  buffer or the 1023 lines are refused with "Buffer full", as a paste is, and the text typed stays once; vim puts them
  all in. See vim-gaps.md.

  u of 2cc or more (and 2S) - Returns to the column the change was typed at, on the first line put back. Vim puts
  the cursor on the second line, at that column if the line is long enough.

  u after insert mode - Typing after a change command (c, s, C, S, cc), and a Backspace or Delete that deletes text
  from before where the typing began (the line break o opened included) or after the cursor clear the undo, so u
  does nothing and the edit before the insert can no longer be undone either. Vim undoes those with the rest of the
  typing (the stretch since the last cursor move, as here), and the change command that began it, as one change. A
  stretch of 256 characters or more is undone but not redone (u again does nothing). The redo of o (u u) puts the
  cursor on the line o opened; vim puts it on the line o was typed on. See vim-gaps.md.

  Counts over 255 - w, b, e, x, X, ~, J, Ctrl-F, Ctrl-B and the word operators (dw, cw, yw, ...) take a count of 256
  or more as 255, so 300w moves 255 words and 300x deletes 255 characters. Vi takes the whole count (j, k, h, l, Space
  and Backspace do here too).

  J - Replaces each line break with one space and keeps the joined line's leading blanks: "abc" and "  def" join as
  "abc   def". Vim removes the leading blanks and puts one space in their place, none when the joined line is empty or
  starts with ')' or the line ends in a blank, and two after a '.', '!' or '?' (vim's default 'joinspaces'): "abc def".
  See vim-gaps.md.

  J with a count over 129 - Is refused with "Too many lines to join", as the joins are kept for undo in one 256-byte
  page. Vi joins them all.

  r with a count over 255 - Replaces 255 characters (r<Enter> too: 300r<Enter> takes 255 characters into the line
  break), as the replaced characters are kept for undo in one 256-byte page. Vi replaces them all.

  r with a control key - r takes a control key as the replacement, as vim does, but Ctrl-H arrives as Backspace and
  cancels r (vim replaces with it; Backspace cancels there too). After Ctrl-V, Enter and Ctrl-J still make a line
  break (vim puts a CR or a NUL character), and vim's character codes (Ctrl-V 065, Ctrl-V x41) are not read: the key
  after Ctrl-V is the replacement.

  The view

  Long jumps - A jump that lands above the view or below it (G, gg, :N, a count on j or k, n, N, 'a, u) puts the
  cursor line on the top or bottom row, whole lines around it. Vim puts it in the middle of the screen when it lands
  far: at least max(2, TEXT_ROWS / 2 - 1) lines above the top line, or below the view when the lines from the first
  one not shown in full to it, and as many after it, take more than the screen (scroll_cursor_halfway). See vim-gaps.md.

  A line that does not fit at the bottom - The rows of it that fit are shown. Vim shows a row of '@' in their place
  (its default 'display'). See vim-gaps.md.

  A line taller than the screen - With the cursor on it, it keeps its place while its rows to the cursor fit below
  the top line, and is shown from the row that keeps the cursor on screen otherwise. Vim makes it the top line as soon
  as the cursor is on it.

  Command line

  : and / input - Limited to what fits the status row after the prompt: the screen width less 2 characters, and at
  most 127 characters for a : command or a search pattern; further keys are ignored. Vim wraps a longer command
  line onto more rows and takes any length.

  :w with no file name, and :w {file} - Started without a file name, the buffer is called [No Name] and :w writes a
  file of that name. :w {file}, :wq {file}, :w! and :wq! say "Unknown command". Vim says "E32: No file name", takes
  the name from :w {file}, writes a copy with :w {file} in a named buffer, and overwrites another file that exists
  only with ! ("E13"). See vim-gaps.md.

  Long messages - Are cut off one column short of the screen width. Vim wraps them onto more rows and waits for Enter.

  Reports - Commands that delete, yank or shift more than 2 lines (dd, yy, >>, <<, :d, :y, :>, :<, and dw or de made
  linewise) report it as vim does with its default 'report', but a shift says "3 lines shifted" where vim says "3
  lines >ed 1 time" (or "<ed"). Vim also reports "3 more lines" after p and P, "3 fewer lines" and "3 lines yanked"
  after a charwise delete or yank over lines (5dw, 5yw), "--No lines in buffer--" when the last line goes, and after
  u and Ctrl-R always ("1 line less; before #2  0 seconds ago"); the editor says nothing there. A : command that
  reports nothing leaves the status bar on the status row, where vim leaves the command line typed.

  Search messages - A search that goes on past the end of the file, or past its start, says nothing; vim says "search
  hit BOTTOM, continuing at TOP" (or "search hit TOP, continuing at BOTTOM"). A pattern that is not found says
  "Pattern not found: {pattern}", where vim's message starts "E486: ". See vim-gaps.md.

  Blanks in a command line - Blanks before a command letter or in a range (: 5, :1,5 d, :1, 5d) make an unknown
  command. Vim skips them there (blanks after the letter are taken as vim takes them, below).

  Characters after a range command letter - d and y take one register name after it, with blanks or not, which
  changes nothing (there is one register: :1,5d_ still yanks the lines, and any character but a digit is taken, where
  vim refuses those that name no register, such as '.'), and anything more is "Unknown command". Vim takes letters
  right after the command letter as part of its name (:1,5da and :2,5yx are "E492: Not an editor command", while
  :1,5delete and :2,5yank work), a digit there as a count (:1,5d3 deletes lines 5-7), > and < repeated as more shifts
  (:1,5>>), and says "E488: Trailing characters" for what follows a register name.

  Marks

  u of J, of a char delete over line breaks or of typed line breaks - A mark set after the change on the line it made
  (for typing, the line it began on; the marks of the lines typed go, as in vim) stays there; vim's u deletes it (u
  deletes the marks of the lines it replaces, then puts back those that were set when the change began, as u does
  here). After cc and S it is the other way round: vim keeps such a mark, the editor's u unsets it. The redo
  (u u) does not put back the marks its undo deleted (set after o, p or typed line breaks on the lines they added);
  vim's Ctrl-R does.

  :marks - Lists marks a-z with their line and text; vim also shows the column and its special marks. A long list
  shows a screen at a time with "-- More --", as in vim, but there q ends the list and any other key shows the next
  page (vim also takes Enter or j for one more line, b for the previous page and ESC to stop). Each page starts on a
  cleared screen, where vim scrolls. After the list any key returns; vim asks "Press ENTER or type command to
  continue" and runs a command key typed there.

  Not wrong, but worth noting

  ~ - Accepts a count, which matches vim but not traditional vi. This is probably fine — most users expect vim behavior
  now.
